Chapter: SerialBridgeTest
  cites Dev chapter SerialBridge
  cites Foreword chapter CCE

 THIS TEST READS THE WIRE, not a return value, and that is the whole
 reason it can tell the interesting failure from success. `SerialBridge`
 writes to port 1016 after polling 1021, which is exactly what the
 compiler's own output path does, so the harness capturing this test's
 output is capturing the mirror itself. A line that appears below appeared
 on COM1. A chapter that formatted perfectly and never reached the port
 would return the same counts and print nothing.

 THE CCE BOUNDARY IS THE DEFECT WORTH CATCHING and it is invisible from
 inside. Codex Text is CCE, so `char-code 'd'` is 22, not 100. If
 `sb-byte-for` forgot `to-unicode`, every mirrored line would still be
 written, still be counted, and still round-trip through any Codex reader
 -- and would arrive on the wire as control characters. So the test prints
 the pair explicitly (22 becomes 100) AND mirrors a readable word: if the
 conversion were dropped, the word below would not be words.

 THE INBOUND CONVERSION IS THE SAME TRAP RUN BACKWARDS and is asserted as
 the mirror image of the outbound one: outbound turns CCE 22 into byte
 100, so inbound must turn byte 100 back into CCE 22. Stating it that way
 means neither direction can be quietly wrong on its own, and a Text built
 without `from-unicode` would answer 100 here -- a Text whose characters
 are numerically ASCII, which compares unequal to every literal in the
 dispatcher while looking correct in any debugger that prints it.

 The byte counts are asserted because a drop is silent by design. The
 transmit wait is fuel-capped and an undrainable line loses bytes on
 purpose, so the count is the only thing that distinguishes "sent eleven"
 from "sent none and carried on politely".

 We say:

Section: Body

  opening : [Console, Device.Port] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("cce-d: " & show (char-code 'd') & " -> byte " & show (sb-byte-for (char-code 'd')))
    print-line-uni ("cce-e: " & show (char-code 'e') & " -> byte " & show (sb-byte-for (char-code 'e')))
    print-line-uni ("emdash: " & show (sb-byte-for 41464))
    print-line-uni ("eol-cr: " & show (sb-is-eol 13) & " eol-lf: " & show (sb-is-eol 10) & " eol-a: " & show (sb-is-eol 97))
    print-line-uni ("printable-32: " & show (sb-is-printable 32) & " printable-126: " & show (sb-is-printable 126) & " printable-127: " & show (sb-is-printable 127))

    n <- sb-write-line "mirror-hello"
    print-line-uni ("wrote: " & show n)

    f <- sb-write-field "label" "value"
    print-line-uni ("field-wrote: " & show f)

    b <- sb-read-byte
    print-line-uni ("idle-read: " & show b)

    print-line-uni ("inbound-100: " & show (char-code (char-at (sb-char-for 100) 0)))
    print-line-uni ("append: [" & sb-append-byte "me" 109 & "]")
    print-line-uni ("append-control: [" & sb-append-byte "me" 7 & "]")
  end
